    "findings": [
      {
        "severity": "minor",
        "file": "task-management/lib/worktree.mjs",
        "line": 182,
        "claim": "Criteria 1 and 2 rest on an unverified assumption about graft's internals: that graft skips its rewrite, including opencode.json, when graft/.cache/wiring-stamp.json names the running version. The seeded version is a guess (the newest among checkout stamps, node_modules and every ~/.npm/_npx entry), so a mismatched running graft still rewrites. The test asserts on the stamp and on a clean tree before graft runs, not after a graft session start.",
        "evidence": "seedGraftStamp picks the newest version across sources. test-worktree.sh:232-236 asserts on the stamp contents and an empty status immediately after createWorktree.",
        "fix": "In a follow-up, capture one real worker session start in a fresh worktree with graft installed and confirm `git status --porcelain` is empty. For robustness against version mismatch, also add graft's machine-local outputs (opencode.json) to the worktree's local exclude."
      },
      {
        "severity": "note",
        "file": "agent-orchestration/topology/lib/management.mjs",
        "line": 125,
        "claim": "I reviewed revision 25d77fcd (patch sha256 d892f44f…), the same patch as request ec45dd7a, which was blocked for checks and scope. task-management/CHANGELOG.md and tests/test-worktree.sh are now in the touches, and all seven required checks are satisfied with host-run results, plus a run of test-worktree.sh. Criterion 3 is met: the finish check uses integrate's foreignDirtyPaths filter and names the dirty paths, with a test. seedGraftStamp is best effort and never fails worktree creation."
      }
